 ],
 "digest": "This week's digest examines institutional mechanisms through which financial centres and devices operate across the Global South, with particular attention to sovereignty, market integration, and professional networks. Tulane's study of monetary control in India and research on African frontier-market liquidity directly address the structural constraints shaping Southern financial autonomy, while investigations of Indonesia's block-trade governance and credit-rating agencies' assessment of Chinese debt exposure reveal the technical and evaluative apparatus through which subordination dynamics unfold. Complementing these empirical analyses, recent regulatory innovations in Casablanca's fintech framework and the BRICS green-bond market's evolution illustrate how Southern financial centres develop their own governance devices, even as evidence from bank performance in Ghana and sovereign debt restructuring in Kenya and Lesotho demonstrates the fragility underlying such institutional projects. We also feature reporting on pan-African banking networks and payments infrastructure, which trace the professional and technical circulation sustaining regional financial integration."
}